1
00:00:00,390 --> 00:00:03,107
Max slams a shoebox on the cafeteria table.

2
00:00:03,847 --> 00:00:07,629
Limited edition. Two hundred dollars. My dad basically owns the mall.

3
00:00:07,868 --> 00:00:08,868
You look at Max.

4
00:00:09,609 --> 00:00:10,816
Then you look a little higher.

5
00:00:11,349 --> 00:00:15,250
Right above his head, floating like a video game health bar, there's a number.

6
00:00:15,490 --> 00:00:17,231
Three dollars and twelve cents.

7
00:00:17,972 --> 00:00:21,486
And under it, a tiny receipt. Pay in four. One of four paid.

8
00:00:21,725 --> 00:00:24,001
Max catches you staring at the air above his head.

9
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:25,628
What? Is there something in my hair?

10
00:00:25,981 --> 00:00:26,981
No, you say.

11
00:00:27,721 --> 00:00:28,721
Nice shoes.

12
00:00:29,460 --> 00:00:33,179
Max nods like a king. Three dollars and twelve cents nods with him.

13
00:00:33,829 --> 00:00:36,201
You're fifteen. Most people look at faces.

14
00:00:36,442 --> 00:00:37,500
You see balances.

15
00:00:38,182 --> 00:00:42,698
Everyone you meet has a number floating over their head. What they really have. Today. Right now.

16
00:00:42,939 --> 00:00:45,246
No glasses. No app. Just numbers.

17
00:00:45,896 --> 00:00:47,253
At first, it's amazing.

18
00:00:47,636 --> 00:00:51,746
Your little sister Mia swears she has no money to chip in for Mom's birthday cake.

19
00:00:51,985 --> 00:00:53,820
Above her head: forty-three dollars.

20
00:00:54,060 --> 00:00:55,623
Check the piggy bank under your bed.

21
00:00:55,863 --> 00:00:57,593
She stares at you like you're a wizard.

22
00:00:57,832 --> 00:00:58,832
Then she pays.

23
00:00:59,573 --> 00:01:03,280
Mom gets her cake. Mia gets her first lesson in hiding money better.

24
00:01:03,521 --> 00:01:07,596
At the corner store, the guy with the gold watch and the loud car has a red number.

25
00:01:07,835 --> 00:01:09,334
Minus nine thousand.

26
00:01:09,575 --> 00:01:14,722
The quiet janitor at your school, Mr. Ortiz, who has worn the same jacket for ten years?

27
00:01:14,962 --> 00:01:16,657
Almost four hundred thousand dollars.

28
00:01:17,602 --> 00:01:18,935
He waves at you with his mop.

29
00:01:19,343 --> 00:01:21,855
You wave back. You've never respected a mop more.

30
00:01:22,094 --> 00:01:24,742
Grandma Rose says she lives on just her little pension.

31
00:01:24,981 --> 00:01:28,688
Her number is so long it doesn't fit above her head. It wraps around her bun.

32
00:01:28,929 --> 00:01:29,929
She winks.

33
00:01:30,668 --> 00:01:32,146
Don't tell your father.

34
00:01:32,409 --> 00:01:35,590
Mom asks if you want the cheap cereal or the good one.

35
00:01:35,829 --> 00:01:39,518
Above her head, the number isn't big. It's fine. Just fine.

36
00:01:39,759 --> 00:01:40,804
You pick the cheap one.

37
00:01:41,498 --> 00:01:44,679
She smiles like she passed a test she didn't know she was taking.

38
00:01:44,920 --> 00:01:48,972
At the school store, a senior tries to sell you a vintage hoodie for sixty dollars.

39
00:01:49,212 --> 00:01:52,420
His number is a hundred and twenty. He really needs the sixty.

40
00:01:52,659 --> 00:01:55,774
You buy it anyway. It's the first time a number has made you kind.

41
00:01:56,015 --> 00:01:58,465
Max, meanwhile, keeps buying things.

42
00:01:58,704 --> 00:02:03,170
Wireless earbuds. A gaming chair he shows you on video. Pizza for the whole table.

43
00:02:03,411 --> 00:02:07,802
Every time, his number blinks a little lower. Every time, he looks a little happier.

44
00:02:08,044 --> 00:02:09,716
Every time, you say nothing.

45
00:02:09,955 --> 00:02:10,955
And then there's Lena.

46
00:02:11,694 --> 00:02:15,850
Lena sits by the window in math and draws tiny planets in the margins.

47
00:02:16,092 --> 00:02:18,054
You decide you will never look above Lena's head.

48
00:02:18,294 --> 00:02:20,825
It's the most romantic decision you have ever made.

49
00:02:21,474 --> 00:02:24,504
If you'd never look either, you're the kind of person who subscribes.

50
00:02:25,155 --> 00:02:26,246
Then it gets bigger.

51
00:02:26,985 --> 00:02:30,236
At the mall, you stop looking at stores and start looking at people.

52
00:02:30,477 --> 00:02:32,706
The nicest outfits have the smallest numbers.

53
00:02:32,945 --> 00:02:36,591
A woman with four shopping bags: minus two thousand three hundred.

54
00:02:36,831 --> 00:02:41,312
A man in a suit, buying a coffee with a credit card: minus sixty thousand.

55
00:02:41,552 --> 00:02:45,008
You start a notebook. Looks rich in one column. Is rich in the other.

56
00:02:45,248 --> 00:02:48,243
After one Saturday, the columns almost never match.

57
00:02:48,688 --> 00:02:53,831
Then there's the guy in flip-flops at the bank, depositing a check with a pen he brought from home.

58
00:02:54,072 --> 00:02:55,454
Biggest number on the street.

59
00:02:55,812 --> 00:03:01,501
On TV, a guy who calls himself the Millionaire Mindset is selling a course for nine hundred ninety-seven dollars.

60
00:03:01,741 --> 00:03:02,741
His number is red.

61
00:03:03,480 --> 00:03:06,561
His rented Lamborghini has its own number. Also red.

62
00:03:06,801 --> 00:03:09,298
You go to a car dealership with Dad, just to look.

63
00:03:09,538 --> 00:03:12,348
For a guy like you, sir, it's only four hundred a month.

64
00:03:12,587 --> 00:03:15,049
Above the salesman's head, the number is great.

65
00:03:15,288 --> 00:03:17,680
Above every customer's head, it's going down.

66
00:03:17,920 --> 00:03:20,451
His whole job is moving numbers from their heads to his.

67
00:03:20,692 --> 00:03:23,052
You try the internet. It doesn't work through a screen.

68
00:03:23,293 --> 00:03:27,557
Only in real life, only in person. So online, everyone is rich again.

69
00:03:27,796 --> 00:03:30,373
And the more numbers you see, the more you notice one thing.

70
00:03:30,615 --> 00:03:31,753
A lot of them are tiny.

71
00:03:32,354 --> 00:03:38,381
In 2025, the Federal Reserve asked Americans what they would do if they suddenly had to pay four hundred dollars.

72
00:03:38,620 --> 00:03:41,012
About four in ten couldn't cover it with cash.

73
00:03:42,060 --> 00:03:45,813
Four in ten. In the cafeteria, that's almost every other table.

74
00:03:46,054 --> 00:03:47,783
You also learn that average lies.

75
00:03:48,024 --> 00:03:53,189
If you add up everything American families own and divide it, the average family has about a million dollars.

76
00:03:53,431 --> 00:03:57,022
But the family right in the middle? About a hundred and ninety thousand.

77
00:03:57,262 --> 00:04:00,783
A few giant numbers pull the average up. Most people never see them.

78
00:04:01,024 --> 00:04:02,664
You see them now. Every day.

79
00:04:03,314 --> 00:04:04,697
But you can't stop seeing.

80
00:04:05,055 --> 00:04:06,618
At dinner, Dad says,

81
00:04:06,859 --> 00:04:08,254
Business is great, kiddo.

82
00:04:08,598 --> 00:04:13,087
Above his head, a red number. Minus two thousand three hundred. And it's flashing.

83
00:04:13,328 --> 00:04:15,606
Mom looks at Dad. Dad looks at his plate.

84
00:04:15,848 --> 00:04:19,273
Nobody looks at the flashing number, because nobody else can see it.

85
00:04:19,512 --> 00:04:22,782
The next week, Dad buys you the new phone you asked for in October.

86
00:04:23,023 --> 00:04:24,160
Happy early birthday.

87
00:04:24,762 --> 00:04:26,445
The number over his head gets redder.

88
00:04:26,685 --> 00:04:27,685
You say thank you.

89
00:04:28,425 --> 00:04:29,889
It's the hardest thank you of your life.

90
00:04:30,165 --> 00:04:31,872
At school, you make a mistake.

91
00:04:32,112 --> 00:04:35,120
Max is bragging again, so you lean over and whisper,

92
00:04:35,360 --> 00:04:36,557
Dude. Three dollars.

93
00:04:37,100 --> 00:04:38,168
You meant it as a joke.

94
00:04:38,839 --> 00:04:40,639
By lunch, the whole table knows.

95
00:04:40,879 --> 00:04:45,012
By the end of the day, someone has written three dollars twelve on Max's locker.

96
00:04:45,252 --> 00:04:46,786
Max doesn't sit with you on Monday.

97
00:04:47,024 --> 00:04:48,024
Or Tuesday.

98
00:04:48,764 --> 00:04:51,295
You find him behind the gym and try to say sorry.

99
00:04:51,536 --> 00:04:52,708
You think I don't know my number?

100
00:04:53,276 --> 00:04:55,951
Everybody at home knows it. That's why I bought the shoes.

101
00:04:56,190 --> 00:04:57,432
He doesn't let you finish.

102
00:04:57,930 --> 00:05:00,647
You stand there with perfect vision, and you can't see a thing.

103
00:05:00,887 --> 00:05:03,105
And it gets worse, because you can't turn it off.

104
00:05:03,345 --> 00:05:06,205
You start sorting people. Rich. Fake. Broke. Fine.

105
00:05:06,444 --> 00:05:11,413
You catch yourself being nicer to the kid with the big number, and shorter with the kid with the small one.

106
00:05:11,653 --> 00:05:12,653
Lena notices.

107
00:05:13,394 --> 00:05:18,251
You keep looking over everyone's head. Like you're reading price tags.

108
00:05:18,492 --> 00:05:19,492
I'm not.

109
00:05:20,232 --> 00:05:22,477
She doesn't believe you. You don't believe you either.

110
00:05:22,716 --> 00:05:24,658
And then you find out something about keeping up.

111
00:05:24,899 --> 00:05:27,709
Researchers looked at people whose neighbor won the lottery.

112
00:05:28,338 --> 00:05:32,043
The bigger the prize next door, the more likely the neighbors were to go bankrupt.

113
00:05:32,283 --> 00:05:34,365
They didn't win anything. They just tried to keep up.

114
00:05:35,723 --> 00:05:37,685
Max wasn't lying because he's a liar.

115
00:05:37,925 --> 00:05:40,228
Max was keeping up with you. With all of you.

116
00:05:40,877 --> 00:05:43,362
The class trip to the coast costs four hundred dollars.

117
00:05:43,601 --> 00:05:45,389
The sign-up sheet goes around the room.

118
00:05:45,629 --> 00:05:48,497
Above half the heads, the number is smaller than four hundred.

119
00:05:48,737 --> 00:05:51,016
Nobody says it. Everybody just says,

120
00:05:51,257 --> 00:05:53,141
Oh, I think I'm busy that weekend.

121
00:05:53,380 --> 00:05:56,588
Max says he's busy. Mia says her friends are all busy.

122
00:05:56,829 --> 00:05:59,708
And then, without meaning to, you look above Lena's head.

123
00:05:59,947 --> 00:06:00,947
You see her number.

124
00:06:01,687 --> 00:06:04,988
Sixty-two dollars. She's been saying she's busy too.

125
00:06:05,228 --> 00:06:09,865
And then, for the first time, you see your own. It's floating above you in the window's reflection.

126
00:06:10,105 --> 00:06:11,115
Eleven dollars.

127
00:06:11,855 --> 00:06:15,769
You've been reading everyone else's numbers for months, and you never once looked at yours.

128
00:06:16,007 --> 00:06:20,605
That night you sit on the stairs and hear Mom and Dad in the kitchen, whispering about the card.

129
00:06:20,846 --> 00:06:22,516
I just didn't want him to feel poor.

130
00:06:22,755 --> 00:06:25,784
He's not poor. We're just pretending.

131
00:06:26,435 --> 00:06:29,256
On Friday, the school holds an assembly about the trip.

132
00:06:29,495 --> 00:06:32,541
Eight hundred kids in one gym. Eight hundred numbers.

133
00:06:32,781 --> 00:06:35,161
The principal asks if anyone has a question.

134
00:06:35,401 --> 00:06:37,596
Your hand goes up before your brain can stop it.

135
00:06:38,336 --> 00:06:39,497
You walk to the microphone.

136
00:06:40,076 --> 00:06:41,760
I can see everyone's bank account.

137
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:43,334
Eight hundred kids laugh.

138
00:06:43,740 --> 00:06:47,494
I'm serious. I won't say anybody's number. But here's what I see.

139
00:06:47,733 --> 00:06:50,454
The newest shoes in this room have some of the smallest numbers.

140
00:06:50,694 --> 00:06:52,776
The quietest jackets have some of the biggest.

141
00:06:53,016 --> 00:06:58,100
And almost half of you, right now, couldn't pay four hundred dollars for this trip without asking someone.

142
00:06:58,338 --> 00:06:59,338
The gym goes quiet.

143
00:07:00,079 --> 00:07:04,216
Eight hundred numbers. And for once, nobody is checking anyone else's.

144
00:07:04,456 --> 00:07:10,245
So here's my idea. Everyone puts in what they can. Anonymously. A box by the door. No names.

145
00:07:10,483 --> 00:07:12,212
And nobody says they're busy anymore.

146
00:07:12,452 --> 00:07:15,308
Somewhere in the back, someone laughs, then stops.

147
00:07:15,548 --> 00:07:18,125
The principal reaches for the microphone, then doesn't.

148
00:07:18,365 --> 00:07:20,026
For a second, nothing happens.

149
00:07:20,766 --> 00:07:27,028
Then Mr. Ortiz, the janitor, walks to the front, takes out his wallet, and drops something into the box.

150
00:07:27,267 --> 00:07:30,042
Then Grandma Rose, who came to watch, does the same.

151
00:07:30,283 --> 00:07:32,458
Then the kid with the gold sneakers. Then Lena.

152
00:07:32,697 --> 00:07:35,925
Then Max, holding three dollars and twelve cents in coins.

153
00:07:36,165 --> 00:07:37,954
The receipt prints and prints.

154
00:07:38,805 --> 00:07:41,209
By Monday, every kid who wants to go is going.

155
00:07:41,449 --> 00:07:43,636
Nobody else can see the numbers. Only you.

156
00:07:43,875 --> 00:07:46,452
And all over the gym, the red ones are going up.

157
00:07:46,692 --> 00:07:50,272
Not because anyone got richer. Because nobody has to pretend anymore.

158
00:07:50,923 --> 00:07:54,197
Back in the cafeteria, Max puts his old sneakers on the table.

159
00:07:54,437 --> 00:07:58,394
I returned them. Three payments left. My dad was so relieved he cried.

160
00:07:58,634 --> 00:07:59,759
You don't look above his head.

161
00:08:00,374 --> 00:08:01,374
You don't need to.

162
00:08:02,113 --> 00:08:04,574
At dinner, Dad tells you the truth about the card.

163
00:08:04,814 --> 00:08:06,348
You give him your eleven dollars.

164
00:08:07,454 --> 00:08:09,404
He laughs so hard he has to sit down.

165
00:08:09,644 --> 00:08:13,983
Then, together, you make a plan. Same number, every Friday. Into a jar.

166
00:08:14,223 --> 00:08:17,253
Mia slides two dollars from her piggy bank across the table.

167
00:08:17,494 --> 00:08:18,494
For the jar.

168
00:08:19,233 --> 00:08:22,395
Grandma Rose finally tells you her big secret. There isn't one.

169
00:08:22,636 --> 00:08:28,457
Same amount, every month, for fifty years. Boring is my superpower.

170
00:08:28,697 --> 00:08:30,903
Lena sits down next to you with her planets.

171
00:08:31,142 --> 00:08:32,730
So, what's my number?

172
00:08:32,971 --> 00:08:34,371
I don't know. I stopped looking.

173
00:08:34,711 --> 00:08:37,082
That's a lie. But it's the best one you ever told.

174
00:08:37,322 --> 00:08:38,680
Here is the one rule you keep.

175
00:08:39,062 --> 00:08:43,280
Don't compare your inside to someone else's outside. Compare this month to last month.

176
00:08:43,520 --> 00:08:46,829
Your number only means something next to your own number from last month.

177
00:08:47,070 --> 00:08:50,967
Your number is going up. Slowly. It's the first number you've ever been proud of.

178
00:08:51,616 --> 00:08:53,613
So... would you want to see everyone's bank account?

179
00:08:53,854 --> 00:08:55,479
And whose number would you look at first?
